Aren't some of the bugs in the buglists "Fixed," rather than "fixed," i.e., 
fixed in the next major revision, not in the next bugfix version? That would 
indicate that some present issues are anticipated as being addressed by 
changes in the game, rather than adjustments in the present construct.

> Perhaps in the far future there will be a 3.5.0 which incorporates
> some of the more popular requests, i.e. a new scoring system, shorter
> Gehennom, harder endgame, more Sokobans, bigrooms and special levels,
> and discouragement of timesink behaviour like pudding farming and
> extinctionism.

This list rather belies the "almost perfect" description of the present 
game. I think people want a more interesting Gehennom, not necessarily 
shorter. I hope there are *not* more Sokobans; Sokoban is bearable now 
because I've got the solutions more or less memorized. If I wanted to play a 
puzzle game I'd play a puzzle game. If more special levels means variations 
on levels swapped in and out (like the present Minetown and Mine's End), I'm 
all for that (new VotD, anyone?); if it means more static levels that are 
the same every time you play, then, no.

I don't think "timesink" behavior should be penalized; the boredom is its 
own discouragement for most players. Let those with infinite patience reap 
the rewards. (Adjusting *tournaments* to reward difficult, as opposed to 
merely tedious, gameplay, is good, though.)

